// FiqhHub — Miscellaneous Rulings Fatwas
window.FATWAS_MISC = [
{id:'ms001',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking & Vaping',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on smoking cigarettes?',a:'If smoking causes significant harm to health (as medical science has established), it is haram on the basis of the prohibition against harming oneself. For those who are addicted, quitting is obligatory and they should use available means to stop. Starting to smoke when one has never smoked is haram. The ruling applies to all tobacco products. Vaping and e-cigarettes are evaluated similarly — if harmful, they are prohibited.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['smoking','cigarettes','vaping','haram','health']},
{id:'ms002',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking & Vaping',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on smoking cigarettes?',a:'Smoking is haram due to its proven health harms. This is a clear fatwa. Starting to smoke is absolutely prohibited. Those already addicted must make serious efforts to quit. Vaping is treated the same as smoking regarding health assessment. Hookah/shisha is similarly prohibited. The principle: whatever medical science definitively proves harmful to the body is haram.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['smoking','cigarettes','vaping','haram','health']},
{id:'ms003',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking & Vaping',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on smoking cigarettes?',a:'Smoking is haram based on the principle of "la darar wa la dirar" (no harm and no causing harm). This includes: cigarettes, cigars, hookah, chewing tobacco, and e-cigarettes if harmful. The social harms (secondhand smoke, financial waste, setting bad example) add to the prohibition. Spending money on cigarettes when it could be used for family needs is additionally problematic.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['smoking','cigarettes','vaping','haram','health']},
{id:'ms004',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'sistani',q:'Are tattoos permissible in Islam?',a:'Getting a tattoo is not intrinsically haram in Shia fiqh. However, conditions apply: (1) It must not prevent water from reaching the skin during wudu/ghusl — if the tattoo is under the skin (as modern tattoos are), it does not create a barrier and wudu is valid. (2) The image must not be haram content, (3) The process must not involve significant harm. Note: this differs from the Sunni position which generally considers tattoos haram.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['tattoo','wudu','skin','permissible','body']},
{id:'ms005',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'khamenei',q:'Are tattoos permissible in Islam?',a:'Tattoos that are injected under the skin (most modern tattoos) do not prevent water reaching the skin, so wudu and ghusl remain valid. Getting a tattoo is permissible if: the content is not haram (no inappropriate images), it doesn\'t involve excessive harm, and it\'s not of religious symbols that would be disrespected. However, it is makruh (discouraged) in general.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['tattoo','wudu','skin','permissible','body']},
{id:'ms006',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'makarem',q:'Are tattoos permissible in Islam?',a:'Modern tattoos (where ink is deposited under the skin layer) do not invalidate wudu as water still reaches the skin surface. The permissibility depends on: (1) content — Islamic, decent content is fine; haram imagery is forbidden, (2) the process — if it involves significant pain or health risk beyond normal, it\'s problematic, (3) social perception — if it leads to negative assumptions about the person\'s faith, caution is advised.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['tattoo','wudu','skin','permissible','body']},
{id:'ms007',cat:'misc',sub:'Pets',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rulings on keeping pets?',a:'Keeping pets is generally permissible with conditions: (1) Dogs — their saliva and wet body are najis. Keeping dogs indoors is makruh except for guarding, herding, or other legitimate purposes. (2) Cats — fully tahir (pure), and the Prophet (s) was known to love cats. (3) Birds, fish, rabbits — permissible. (4) All pets must be properly fed and cared for — neglecting an animal is sinful. (5) Money spent on pets should not be excessive when there are greater obligations unfulfilled.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['pets','dogs','cats','animals','najis','permissible']},
{id:'ms008',cat:'misc',sub:'Pets',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the rulings on keeping pets?',a:'Keeping animals requires proper care — Islam takes animal rights seriously. Dogs are najis in body moisture but keeping them for security, herding, hunting, or assistance (guide dogs) is permissible. Cats are encouraged — they are tahir and beloved in Islamic tradition. Fish, birds, and other clean animals make good pets. Animal cruelty in any form is haram.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['pets','dogs','cats','animals','najis','permissible']},
{id:'ms009',cat:'misc',sub:'Pets',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rulings on keeping pets?',a:'The key principle is responsible pet ownership. Islam requires: adequate food and water, proper shelter, veterinary care when needed, and not causing the animal distress. Keeping exotic animals that belong in the wild is discouraged. The animal\'s welfare must be genuinely considered. Imam Sadiq (as) narrated that a woman entered hellfire for starving a cat — showing how seriously Islam takes animal welfare.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['pets','dogs','cats','animals','najis','permissible']},
{id:'ms010',cat:'misc',sub:'Organ Donation',marja:'sistani',q:'Is organ donation permissible?',a:'Organ donation is permissible if: (1) It saves a life or significantly improves quality of life, (2) The donor consents (or their family for deceased donors), (3) For living donors — the donation must not cause death or serious harm, (4) For deceased donors — death must be medically confirmed. The principle of saving life (which is likened in Quran to saving all humanity) supports organ donation. It is actually recommended when it saves lives.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['organ','donation','transplant','life','saving']},
{id:'ms011',cat:'misc',sub:'Organ Donation',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is organ donation permissible?',a:'Organ donation from deceased persons is permissible and can be meritorious if it saves lives. Living donation is permissible for renewable organs/tissues (blood, bone marrow) and for non-vital organs (one kidney) if the risk is acceptable. Selling organs is problematic — donation should be voluntary. Brain death as a criterion for organ harvesting is accepted by most contemporary scholars.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['organ','donation','transplant','life','saving']},
{id:'ms012',cat:'misc',sub:'Organ Donation',marja:'makarem',q:'Is organ donation permissible?',a:'Organ donation is one of the greatest acts of charity. The Quran says: "Whoever saves a life, it is as if they saved all of humanity" (5:32). Both living and posthumous donation are permissible. The donor or their family must consent. Commercial organ trade is prohibited to protect the vulnerable. Blood donation is highly encouraged.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['organ','donation','transplant','life','saving']},
{id:'ms013',cat:'misc',sub:'Photography',marja:'sistani',q:'Is photography and video permissible?',a:'Photography and video are permissible. The prohibition in some classical texts related to sculpting three-dimensional idols (taswir), which was connected to idol worship. Modern photography, video, and digital imaging are different in nature and are halal. Conditions: (1) The content must not be haram (inappropriate images), (2) Photography of non-mahram should respect hijab norms, (3) Using images for deception is haram.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['photography','video','images','permissible','taswir']},
{id:'ms014',cat:'misc',sub:'Photography',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is photography and video permissible?',a:'Photography, filmmaking, and digital media are halal tools. They can be used for education, documentation, art, and communication. The earlier scholarly discussions about taswir concerned physical statues used in worship. Modern visual media serves legitimate purposes. Content standards apply — the medium is neutral; the content determines the ruling.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['photography','video','images','permissible','taswir']},
{id:'ms015',cat:'misc',sub:'Photography',marja:'makarem',q:'Is photography and video permissible?',a:'Photography and video are permissible and can be means of great good — documenting knowledge, preserving history, education, and communication. The rulings about taswir in hadith were about idol-making, not modern imaging. Using photography for haram purposes (spreading indecent content) is what makes specific uses forbidden, not the technology itself.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['photography','video','images','permissible','taswir']},
{id:'ms016',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on gambling and lottery?',a:'Gambling in all forms is haram — the Quran explicitly prohibits it (5:90-91). This includes: casino gambling, sports betting, poker for money, lottery tickets, scratch cards, and any game where money is wagered on uncertain outcomes. Online gambling is the same ruling. Prizes from competitions based on skill (not chance) are halal. Raffles where a ticket is purchased for a chance to win are debated — if the ticket price includes a charitable donation and the chance of winning is secondary, some scholars show flexibility.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['gambling','lottery','haram','betting','prohibited','games']},
{id:'ms017',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on gambling and lottery?',a:'Gambling is absolutely forbidden. The Quran groups it with alcohol and idolatry as abominations. All forms: casinos, betting shops, online gambling, crypto gambling, sports wagering, and lottery. The harm is clear: addiction, financial ruin, family destruction, and social corruption. Prizes from genuine competitions (academic, athletic, skill-based) are halal because the outcome depends on effort and ability, not mere chance.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','lottery','haram','betting','prohibited','games']},
{id:'ms018',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on gambling and lottery?',a:'Gambling is one of the clear-cut prohibitions in Islam. Modern forms include: online poker, fantasy sports with money, cryptocurrency gambling, prediction markets with stakes, and even some stock options that function as gambling. The test: is the outcome primarily determined by chance with money at stake? If yes, it is gambling. Skill-based competitions with prizes are different — entering a coding contest, academic olympiad, or sports tournament for prizes is halal.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','lottery','haram','betting','prohibited','games']},
{id:'ms019',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'sistani',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'Islam emphasizes stewardship (khilafah) of the earth. Humans are trustees, not owners, of the natural world. Key principles: (1) Waste is prohibited — "Eat and drink but waste not" (7:31), (2) Water conservation — even during wudu by a river, one should not waste water, (3) Planting trees is an ongoing charity, (4) Pollution that harms others\' health is prohibited under "la darar" (no harm), (5) Hunting endangered species is problematic, (6) Destroying crops and livestock in war is forbidden. These principles strongly support modern environmental protection.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['environment','stewardship','protection','waste','islam','conservation']},
{id:'ms020',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'makarem',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'Environmental stewardship is a fundamental Islamic value. The Prophet (s) said: "If the Day of Judgment arrives and you have a seedling in your hand, plant it." Key environmental ethics: (1) Don\'t waste resources — water, food, energy, (2) Don\'t cause fasad fil-ard (corruption on earth), (3) Every living creature has rights, (4) Future generations have a claim on current resources. Climate change, pollution, and environmental destruction are forms of fasad that Islam opposes. Muslims should be at the forefront of environmental protection.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','stewardship','protection','waste','islam','conservation']},
{id:'ms021',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'noori',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'The earth is an amanah (trust) from Allah. We will be asked about how we treated it. Islamic environmental principles include: ihsan (excellence) in treating creation, mizan (balance) in using resources, avoiding israf (waste), and maintaining the natural systems Allah created. Imam Ali (as) in Nahj al-Balagha frequently referenced natural phenomena as signs of divine wisdom — destroying these signs is opposing divine will. Conservation, sustainability, and green living align perfectly with Islamic values.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','stewardship','protection','waste','islam','conservation']},
{id:'ms022',cat:'misc',sub:'Charity',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the best forms of charity (sadaqah) in Islam?',a:'The best forms of sadaqah include: (1) Sadaqah jariyah (ongoing charity): building wells, schools, mosques, hospitals, (2) Teaching knowledge — the Prophet (s) said it\'s better than material charity, (3) Helping someone find employment — giving them a means of sustenance, (4) Feeding the hungry — especially during famines and disasters, (5) Caring for orphans — "I and the guardian of an orphan are like this in Paradise" (gesture of two fingers together), (6) Removing harm from the path — even small acts count, (7) Secret charity — giving without anyone knowing is more rewarded.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['charity','sadaqah','giving','orphan','ongoing','best']},
{id:'ms023',cat:'misc',sub:'Charity',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the best forms of charity (sadaqah) in Islam?',a:'Islam has a comprehensive charity framework: (1) Obligatory: khums, zakat, fitrah, (2) Highly recommended: feeding the hungry, helping the needy, sponsoring orphans, (3) Ongoing: waqf properties, educational institutions, water projects, (4) Personal: smiling at others, removing obstacles from paths, good advice, reconciling disputes. The Prophet (s) said: "Every act of kindness is charity." Even a kind word is sadaqah. The spirit is generosity in all forms — material, emotional, and spiritual.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['charity','sadaqah','giving','orphan','ongoing','best']},

{id:'ms024',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'sistani',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'All forms of gambling are absolutely haram in Islam. This includes: casino games, lotteries, sports betting, poker for money, slot machines, and any game where money is wagered on uncertain outcomes. The Quran explicitly prohibits it: "O believers, intoxicants, gambling, idolatry, and divination are an abomination of Satan\'s work — avoid them" (5:90). Games of skill played without betting are permissible. Winnings from gambling are haram money that must be returned or given as charity.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['gambling','haram','lottery','betting','prohibited']},
{id:'ms025',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'Gambling in all forms is haram — no exceptions. This extends to: online gambling, cryptocurrency trading that functions as gambling (pure speculation without underlying value), lottery tickets, raffle tickets with entry fees, and betting on sports events. Prize competitions where entry is free (no financial risk to participants) are generally permissible. The distinction is whether the participant risks their own money on an uncertain outcome.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','haram','lottery','betting','prohibited']},
{id:'ms026',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'makarem',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'The Quran categorically prohibits gambling alongside alcohol and idolatry (5:90-91). This covers all forms: traditional gambling, modern casino games, online betting, lotteries, and speculative schemes. Even small-stakes gambling "for fun" is haram because it cultivates the gambling mentality. Games played for entertainment without money are fine — chess, card games, video games are permissible when played without betting.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','haram','lottery','betting','prohibited']},
{id:'ms027',cat:'misc',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Backbiting (ghibah) is haram and a major sin. It is defined as saying something true about a person behind their back that they would dislike hearing. The Quran compares it to eating the flesh of one\'s dead brother (49:12). It is worse than lying because the victim has no defense. Repentance requires: stopping the behavior, seeking forgiveness from Allah, and ideally seeking forgiveness from the person if it won\'t cause greater harm. Exceptions: warning about someone who is a danger, seeking fatwa about a situation, criticizing public officials\' public conduct.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['backbiting','ghibah','sin','forbidden','social']},
{id:'ms028',cat:'misc',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Ghibah is among the sins most strongly condemned in Quran and hadith. The Prophet (s) said: "Ghibah is worse than zina (fornication) because the fornicator can repent and be forgiven, but the backbiter is not forgiven until the person they backbit forgives them." Limited exceptions exist: warning someone about a danger (nasiha), testimony in court, seeking religious ruling where details are necessary, and criticizing a public figure\'s public actions.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['backbiting','ghibah','sin','forbidden','social']},
{id:'ms029',cat:'misc',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on backbiting (ghibah)?',a:'Backbiting destroys social fabric and is severely condemned. Even listening to ghibah is sinful — one should stop the speaker or leave the gathering. The cure includes: reflecting on one\'s own faults, recognizing the spiritual damage ghibah causes to oneself, and making a firm resolution to speak only good about others. The Prophet (s) said: "Let the one who believes in Allah and the Last Day speak good or remain silent."',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['backbiting','ghibah','sin','forbidden','social']},
{id:'ms030',cat:'misc',sub:'Lying',marja:'sistani',q:'Is lying ever permissible?',a:'Lying is haram in principle. However, specific exceptions exist: (1) To save a life — lying to protect someone from being killed is obligatory, (2) To reconcile between people — using diplomatic language to resolve conflicts, (3) Taqiyyah — concealing one\'s beliefs to protect oneself from persecution, (4) In war (strategic deception against enemies). Even in these cases, if the goal can be achieved without lying, lying should be avoided. Using ambiguous language (tawriyah) that is technically true but misleading is permissible in cases of necessity.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['lying','permissible','exceptions','taqiyyah','truth']},
{id:'ms031',cat:'misc',sub:'Lying',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is lying ever permissible?',a:'Lying is generally haram with recognized exceptions based on maslaha (greater benefit): saving life, preventing harm, reconciliation between people, and taqiyyah in situations of genuine danger. The exceptions are precisely defined — one cannot extend them to everyday convenience. White lies for politeness (like complimenting cooking one doesn\'t enjoy) are a separate discussion — scholars generally permit social courtesy that avoids hurting feelings.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['lying','permissible','exceptions','taqiyyah','truth']},
{id:'ms032',cat:'misc',sub:'Lying',marja:'noori',q:'Is lying ever permissible?',a:'The Prophet (s) said: "Truthfulness leads to righteousness and righteousness leads to Paradise." Lying is fundamentally haram. The exceptions are narrow and well-defined: protecting life, reconciling people, taqiyyah, and marital diplomacy (saying kind things to one\'s spouse). The Imams (as) were known as al-Sadiq (the truthful), emphasizing the centrality of honesty to Islamic character. Habitual lying darkens the heart and destroys trust.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['lying','permissible','exceptions','taqiyyah','truth']},
{id:'ms033',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'sistani',q:'What does Islam say about environmental responsibility?',a:'Islam mandates environmental stewardship (khilafah). Key principles: (1) Humans are trustees (khulafa) of the earth, not owners, (2) Wasting resources (israf) is haram, (3) Polluting water sources is forbidden, (4) Cutting down trees unnecessarily is discouraged, (5) The Prophet (s) said: "If the Day of Judgment comes and you have a seedling in your hand, plant it." Environmental destruction for profit violates the trust (amanah) given to humanity. Conservation is a form of worship.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['environment','conservation','stewardship','pollution','nature']},
{id:'ms034',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'khamenei',q:'What does Islam say about environmental responsibility?',a:'Environmental protection is an Islamic obligation rooted in the concept of divine trust (amanah). Pollution, deforestation, species extinction, and wasteful consumption all violate Islamic principles. Water conservation is specifically emphasized in Islamic law — even wudu water should not be wasted. The current environmental crisis requires Muslims to actively participate in conservation efforts as part of their religious duty.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','conservation','stewardship','pollution','nature']},
{id:'ms035',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'makarem',q:'What does Islam say about environmental responsibility?',a:'The Quran says: "Do not cause corruption on earth after it has been set right" (7:56). Environmental responsibility includes: efficient use of resources, minimizing waste, protecting biodiversity, avoiding pollution, and sustainable agriculture. The Prophet (s) prohibited urinating in water sources, cutting fruit-bearing trees, and wasting water. These 1400-year-old teachings anticipate modern environmental ethics.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','conservation','stewardship','pollution','nature']},

{id:'ms036',cat:'misc',sub:'Istikhara',marja:'sistani',q:'What is istikhara and how is it performed?',a:'Istikhara is seeking Allah\'s guidance when making a decision after proper human deliberation. Methods include: (1) Prayer istikhara — pray 2 rak\'ahs and ask Allah for guidance, then observe if the heart inclines toward or away from the matter, (2) Quran istikhara — pray, then open the Quran randomly and derive guidance, (3) Tasbih istikhara — using prayer beads with a specific process. Istikhara should be used after one has done proper research and consultation — not as a replacement for rational thinking. It is not fortune-telling.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['istikhara','guidance','quran','prayer','decision']},
{id:'ms037',cat:'misc',sub:'Istikhara',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is istikhara and how is it performed?',a:'Istikhara is a spiritual tool used after human effort (research, consultation, deliberation) has been exhausted and one remains undecided. It is not meant for decisions where the answer is clear or for everyday trivial matters. The best method is salat al-istikhara followed by trusting one\'s inclination. Quran istikhara through a scholar is also common. The result should be accepted with trust in Allah. Repeatedly doing istikhara for the same question is inappropriate.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['istikhara','guidance','quran','prayer','decision']},
{id:'ms038',cat:'misc',sub:'Istikhara',marja:'makarem',q:'What is istikhara and how is it performed?',a:'Istikhara has specific etiquette: (1) It is for genuinely undecided matters after human deliberation, (2) It should not replace rational analysis, (3) It should not be used for matters where Islamic law provides clear guidance, (4) The result should be respected, (5) It can be done personally or through a scholar. The essence is tawakkul (reliance on Allah) after tying your camel — doing your human best first.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['istikhara','guidance','quran','prayer','decision']},
{id:'ms039',cat:'misc',sub:'Dreams',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic perspective on dreams?',a:'Dreams in Islam fall into three categories: (1) True dreams (ru\'ya sadiqah) — genuine divine communication, more common for the righteous, especially near dawn, (2) Self-reflective dreams — arising from one\'s thoughts, fears, and experiences, (3) Disturbing dreams — from shaytan. No legal ruling can be derived from dreams — they cannot make something halal or haram. Prophetic dreams were exclusive to prophets. If one has a good dream, they should praise Allah; if bad, seek refuge in Allah and not share it. Dream interpretation is not an exact science.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dreams','interpretation','vision','sleep','islamic']},
{id:'ms040',cat:'misc',sub:'Dreams',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the Islamic perspective on dreams?',a:'Islam acknowledges dreams as potentially meaningful but warns against over-reliance. The Prophet (s) said true dreams are 1/46th of prophethood. However: dreams cannot be used to derive religious rulings, they should not override reason and knowledge, interpretation requires expertise, and disturbing dreams should be dismissed. The best approach is: thank Allah for good dreams, seek protection from bad ones, and make decisions based on rational analysis and Islamic guidance rather than dream content.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['dreams','interpretation','vision','sleep','islamic']},
{id:'ms041',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'sistani',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'All forms of gambling are haram. This includes: casino games, card games for money, sports betting, lottery, slot machines, and any game where money is wagered on uncertain outcomes. Even watching gambling or being present in gambling venues is makruh. The Quran groups gambling with alcohol as "abominations from the work of Satan" (5:90). Games of skill without gambling (chess, cards without wagering) are discussed separately.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['gambling','qimar','haram','betting','lottery']},
{id:'ms042',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'Gambling in all forms is haram without exception. This extends to: online gambling, fantasy sports with money stakes, prediction markets, and any scheme where money is risked on chance. Lottery tickets are haram. Prize draws where no purchase is required (free raffles) are debated — some scholars permit them as gifts, not gambling. The principle: if money is staked on uncertain outcomes, it is gambling.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','qimar','haram','betting','lottery']},
{id:'ms043',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'makarem',q:'Is gambling (qimar) permissible in any form?',a:'The prohibition of gambling is absolute and includes modern forms: online casinos, cryptocurrency gambling, betting apps, and loot boxes in video games (when real money is involved). The wisdom behind the prohibition: gambling destroys families, creates addiction, redistributes wealth unjustly, and breeds enmity between people. Even "small" bets normalize gambling behavior.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','qimar','haram','betting','lottery']},
{id:'ms044',cat:'misc',sub:'Chess',marja:'sistani',q:'Is playing chess permissible?',a:'Playing chess is permissible if: (1) It is not played for gambling (no betting), (2) It does not cause neglect of religious obligations, (3) It does not consume excessive time that should be spent on beneficial activities. Chess is considered a game of skill and intelligence, not a gambling instrument. This ruling applies to chess as currently played — as a pure strategy game.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['chess','games','permissible','gambling','skill']},
{id:'ms045',cat:'misc',sub:'Chess',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is playing chess permissible?',a:'Chess is permissible under conditions: no gambling, no neglecting duties, and the game is treated as an intellectual exercise. Historically, some scholars prohibited chess because it was associated with gambling and time-wasting. In the modern context where it is recognized as a sport and mental exercise, the prohibition is not applicable as long as the conditions are met.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['chess','games','permissible','gambling','skill']},
{id:'ms046',cat:'misc',sub:'Chess',marja:'makarem',q:'Is playing chess permissible?',a:'Chess as a game of strategy and skill is permissible when played without gambling. It can even be beneficial for developing analytical thinking. However, excessive time spent on any entertainment — including chess — at the expense of worship, work, family, or study is problematic. Balance is the Islamic principle.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['chess','games','permissible','gambling','skill']},
{id:'ms047',cat:'misc',sub:'Plastic Surgery',marja:'sistani',q:'Is cosmetic/plastic surgery permissible?',a:'Cosmetic surgery is permissible. Islam does not prohibit altering one\'s appearance for beautification. This includes: rhinoplasty (nose surgery), facelifts, corrective procedures, and other cosmetic enhancements. The conditions are: (1) The procedure must not involve haram (e.g., gender transition surgery is a separate discussion), (2) It should not cause disproportionate health risk, (3) A non-mahram doctor of opposite gender is permitted for medical necessity when a same-gender doctor is unavailable.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['plastic surgery','cosmetic','permissible','beautification','appearance']},
{id:'ms048',cat:'misc',sub:'Plastic Surgery',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is cosmetic/plastic surgery permissible?',a:'Cosmetic surgery is permissible as a general rule. There is no Islamic basis for claiming it is "changing God\'s creation" in the prohibited sense — the Quranic verse about changing creation refers to following Satan in corrupting human nature, not physical modification. People have the right to improve their appearance. Medical ethics and safety standards must be observed.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['plastic surgery','cosmetic','permissible','beautification','appearance']},
{id:'ms049',cat:'misc',sub:'Plastic Surgery',marja:'makarem',q:'Is cosmetic/plastic surgery permissible?',a:'Beautification is permitted in Islam — the Prophet (s) encouraged looking presentable. Cosmetic surgery falls under this general permission. Conditions: safety, reasonable expectations, and not driven by unhealthy obsession with appearance. Reconstructive surgery after injury or illness is clearly permissible and even praiseworthy. The prohibition applies only if the surgery involves something inherently haram.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['plastic surgery','cosmetic','permissible','beautification','appearance']},
{id:'ms050',cat:'misc',sub:'Adoption',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic view on adoption?',a:'Islam encourages caring for orphans — it is among the most praised acts. However, Islam distinguishes between kafala (guardianship/sponsorship) and tabanni (full adoption that changes lineage). Full adoption that changes the child\'s surname and creates false lineage is not permitted — the child should know their biological parents. However: providing a home, education, love, and care through kafala is highly encouraged. The guardian can give the child inheritance through wasiyyah (up to 1/3).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['adoption','kafala','orphans','guardianship','lineage']},
{id:'ms051',cat:'misc',sub:'Adoption',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the Islamic view on adoption?',a:'Caring for orphans is a major Islamic value — the Quran praises it extensively. The distinction is about lineage, not care. The child keeps their family name and inheritance connections. But in every practical sense, they are part of the family. Mahramiyyah can be established through breastfeeding the child (by the guardian\'s wife or a relative). The guardian family should love and treat the child exactly as their own.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['adoption','kafala','orphans','guardianship','lineage']},
{id:'ms052',cat:'misc',sub:'Adoption',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the Islamic view on adoption?',a:'Islam transformed pre-Islamic adoption practices to protect children\'s identity rights. The child keeps their biological identity while receiving full love and care. Practical implications: the guardian provides everything a parent would — home, education, affection, financial support. The only differences are: lineage preservation and mahram rules (addressed through breastfeeding). Emotionally and practically, Islamic guardianship is parenting in every meaningful sense.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['adoption','kafala','orphans','guardianship','lineage']},
{id:'ms053',cat:'misc',sub:'Music Instruments',marja:'sistani',q:'Is learning to play musical instruments permissible?',a:'Musical instruments used for lahwi (entertainment-type) music are haram to play and listen to. However, instruments used for non-lahwi purposes — such as military marches, religious ceremonies (specific types of drums for Muharram), or therapeutic contexts — are permissible. The classification depends on the common usage and context. Learning instruments for permissible purposes is allowed.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['instruments','music','lahw','permissible','haram']},
{id:'ms054',cat:'misc',sub:'Music Instruments',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is learning to play musical instruments permissible?',a:'Using instruments of lahw (entertainment/revelry) is haram. This includes instruments primarily associated with gatherings of sin. However, instruments used for revolutionary anthems, religious chanting (with appropriate instruments), and martial purposes are permissible. The context and content determine the ruling more than the instrument itself in some views.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['instruments','music','lahw','permissible','haram']},
{id:'ms055',cat:'misc',sub:'Music Instruments',marja:'makarem',q:'Is learning to play musical instruments permissible?',a:'The general rule prohibits instruments of lahw. But the definition of lahw is contextual — an instrument used for dignified, spiritual, or educational purposes may be evaluated differently. Some scholars are more restrictive (all instruments are haram except specific drums for Muharram) while others are more flexible (the content and context matter most). Consult your specific marja\'s detailed risalah on music.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['instruments','music','lahw','permissible','haram']},
{id:'ms056',cat:'misc',sub:'Interest (Riba)',marja:'sistani',q:'Is paying or receiving bank interest permissible?',a:'Riba (interest/usury) is categorically haram. The Quran equates dealing in riba with waging war against Allah (2:279). Rules: (1) Giving interest-based loans is haram, (2) Taking interest-based loans is haram unless absolutely necessary (like buying a home when no alternative exists — the sin falls on the lender), (3) Receiving interest from Islamic banks under Sharia-compliant contracts is halal, (4) Interest received from non-Muslim banks in non-Muslim countries — some scholars permit taking it as it is not riba between Muslims.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['riba','interest','banking','haram','islamic finance']},
{id:'ms057',cat:'misc',sub:'Interest (Riba)',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is paying or receiving bank interest permissible?',a:'Riba is among the most severely prohibited acts. However, practical rulings: deposits in Islamic banks under mudarabah or musharakah contracts are halal — the profit share is not riba. In non-Muslim countries, interest received from banks can be taken as permissible income according to some views, since the prohibition applies to transactions between Muslims. Mortgages in non-Muslim countries may have specific dispensations due to necessity.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['riba','interest','banking','haram','islamic finance']},
{id:'ms058',cat:'misc',sub:'Interest (Riba)',marja:'makarem',q:'Is paying or receiving bank interest permissible?',a:'The prohibition of riba is absolute and repeated in the Quran. Islamic banking alternatives include: murabaha (cost-plus sale), musharakah (partnership), ijarah (leasing), and mudarabah (profit-sharing). Muslims should seek Sharia-compliant financial products. When living in non-Muslim countries with no Islamic banking options, consult your marja for specific guidance on necessity-based exceptions.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['riba','interest','banking','haram','islamic finance']},
{id:'ms059',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'sistani',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'Environmental stewardship is an Islamic duty. Humans are khalifah (stewards) of the earth, not owners. The Quran warns against corruption on earth (fasad fil-ard). Practical obligations include: not wasting natural resources (the Prophet prohibited wasting water even by a flowing river), protecting ecosystems, avoiding pollution, planting trees (considered ongoing charity), and supporting conservation. Environmental destruction for profit violates Islamic principles of trusteeship.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['environment','stewardship','khalifah','conservation','pollution']},
{id:'ms060',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'makarem',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'Islam is inherently environmentalist. Key principles: (1) Humans are trustees, not owners of the earth, (2) Every creation has rights and purpose, (3) Waste (israf) is sinful, (4) Planting trees is sadaqah jariyah, (5) Polluting water sources is haram. The Prophet (s) established the concept of hima — protected natural reserves. Modern environmental challenges require Muslim scholars and communities to articulate a robust Islamic environmentalism.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','stewardship','khalifah','conservation','pollution']},
{id:'ms061',cat:'misc',sub:'Environmental Ethics',marja:'khamenei',q:'What does Islam say about environmental protection?',a:'Environmental protection is a religious obligation rooted in the Quran\'s concept of human stewardship (khilafah). Wasting resources, polluting air and water, deforestation, and contributing to climate change contradict Islamic values. Muslim-majority nations should lead in sustainable development. Individual Muslims should: reduce waste, conserve water, plant trees, and support environmental causes as acts of faith.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','stewardship','khalifah','conservation','pollution']},
,
{id:'ms062',cat:'misc',sub:'Dreams',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic view on dreams?',a:'Dreams are categorized into three types: (1) True dreams (ruya sadiqah) — from Allah, often clear and meaningful, (2) Dreams from Shaytan — frightening or disturbing, and (3) Dreams from the self (nafs) — reflecting one\'s thoughts, worries, and experiences. True dreams can be a form of divine communication but should not be used as the basis for Islamic rulings. If one sees a good dream, thank Allah; if bad, seek refuge in Allah.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['dreams','types','true','shaytan']},
{id:'ms063',cat:'misc',sub:'Istikhara',marja:'sistani',q:'What is istikhara and how is it performed?',a:'Istikhara is seeking guidance from Allah when facing a decision after using all rational means. Methods include: (1) The Quran method — praying two rakats, asking Allah, then opening the Quran randomly and interpreting, (2) The tasbih method — using prayer beads with specific count, (3) Simply praying and asking Allah for guidance. Istikhara is for matters where one is genuinely undecided — it should not replace rational analysis.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['istikhara','guidance','quran','decision']},
{id:'ms064',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on tattoos?',a:'Tattoos are not inherently najis and do not prevent wudu or ghusl water from reaching the skin (since the ink is under the skin). However, getting a tattoo involves needless pain and permanent body modification, which some scholars discourage. If the image is of something haram or the tattoo is placed where it must be exposed to non-mahram, additional issues arise. Existing tattoos do not affect the validity of prayers.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['tattoos','wudu','permissible','body']},
{id:'ms065',cat:'misc',sub:'Pets',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on keeping dogs and cats?',a:'Dogs are considered najis in Shia fiqh — their saliva, hair, and body contact create najasah. Keeping dogs inside the home is problematic for maintaining taharat. However, guard dogs, herding dogs, and hunting dogs kept outside are permissible. Cats are tahir and permissible to keep as pets — the Prophet (s) had companions known for their love of cats. Other tahir animals can also be kept as pets.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dogs','cats','pets','najis']},
{id:'ms066',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'makarem',q:'What is Islam\'s position on environmental protection?',a:'Islam mandates environmental stewardship. Humans are khalifah (trustees) of the earth, not owners. The Prophet (s) said: if the Day of Judgment comes and you have a seedling in your hand, plant it. Wasting resources, polluting water and air, unnecessary deforestation, and causing species extinction all contradict Islamic principles. Environmental protection is a religious obligation rooted in the concept of amanah (trust).',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['environment','stewardship','khalifah','trust']},
{id:'ms067',cat:'misc',sub:'Superstition',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Islam\'s view on superstitions?',a:'Islam rejects superstitions and baseless beliefs. Belief in omens, lucky/unlucky numbers, astrology for predicting the future, and similar practices contradicts tawhid (monotheism) — as only Allah determines outcomes. The Prophet (s) said there is no bad omen in Islam. However, some practices classified as \'superstition\' may actually be recommended acts (like istikhara) — the distinction is between relying on Allah versus attributing power to created things.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['superstition','tawhid','omens','astrology']}
,
{id:'ms068',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on smoking?',a:'Many contemporary scholars have moved toward prohibiting smoking due to its proven severe health harms. At minimum, starting to smoke is haram due to the certain harm it causes. For current smokers, quitting is strongly recommended and arguably obligatory. Smoking near others (secondhand smoke) that harms them is also prohibited. The cost of cigarettes, when it constitutes waste (israf), adds another dimension of prohibition.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['smoking','health','harm','prohibited']},
{id:'ms069',cat:'misc',sub:'Names',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the rules for naming children in Islam?',a:'Choosing good names is a right of the child. Recommended names: names of the Prophet (s) and Imams (a) — Muhammad, Ali, Hasan, Husayn, Fatimah, Zaynab. Names of prophets are also recommended. Prohibited: names that carry bad or ugly meanings, names exclusive to Allah (al-Khaliq, al-Razzaq without Abd prefix). Using Abdul with Allah\'s names (Abdullah, Abdur-Rahman) is highly recommended. The name should carry dignity and positive meaning.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['names','children','recommended','prohibited']},
{id:'ms070',cat:'misc',sub:'Photography',marja:'sistani',q:'Is photography and video recording permissible?',a:'Photography and video recording are permissible in themselves — they are not the same as sculpture/idol-making which was historically prohibited. However, the content must be appropriate: photographing/recording haram activities, taking immodest photos, or recording without consent can be problematic for separate reasons. Portrait photography, family photos, educational videos, and documentaries are all fine.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['photography','video','permissible','content']},
{id:'ms071',cat:'misc',sub:'Tawassul',marja:'sistani',q:'What is tawassul and is it permissible?',a:'Tawassul is seeking proximity to Allah through an intermediary — the Prophet (s), the Imams (a), or other righteous figures. It is supported by Quranic evidence (5:35 — seek means of approach to Him) and authenticated hadith. One is not worshipping the intermediary but asking Allah through their elevated status. It is similar to asking a living person to pray for you. Shia scholars unanimously consider it permissible and recommended.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['tawassul','intermediary','permissible','quran']},
{id:'ms072',cat:'misc',sub:'Ziyarah',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the significance of visiting shrines of the Imams?',a:'Visiting the shrines of the Prophet (s) and Imams (a) is highly recommended and carries immense spiritual reward. Numerous hadith encourage ziyarah — visiting Imam Husayn (a) in Karbala is particularly emphasized. The visit should include: reciting prescribed ziyarah texts, praying two rakats, making dua, and reflecting on the Imam\'s teachings and sacrifice. Ziyarah strengthens the bond between the believer and the Ahlul Bayt (a).',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['ziyarah','shrines','recommended','karbala']}
,
{id:'mi062',cat:'misc',sub:'Dreams',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic view on dreams?',a:'Dreams are categorized as: (1) True dreams (ruya sadiqah) — from Allah, often clear and may contain guidance or glad tidings, (2) Dreams from Shaytan — disturbing, confusing, designed to frighten, (3) Self-generated dreams — reflections of daily thoughts and concerns. True dreams are possible for anyone, not limited to prophets. However, dreams should not be used as a basis for legal rulings. Istikhara through dreams has some scholarly support.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dreams','true','shaytan','guidance']},
{id:'mi063',cat:'misc',sub:'Superstition',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Islam\'s position on superstition?',a:'Islam rejects superstition. Belief in bad luck from black cats, broken mirrors, numbers, or days contradicts tawhid — only Allah controls destiny. Fortune-telling, astrology for predicting the future, palm reading, and similar practices are prohibited. However, performing istikhara (seeking divine guidance) through Quran or prayer is a legitimate Islamic practice. Muslims should rely on Allah, rational thinking, and proper planning.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['superstition','prohibited','tawhid','fortune-telling']},
{id:'mi064',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic view on environmental protection?',a:'Humans are khalifah (stewards) of the earth, not its owners. Environmental protection is an Islamic obligation: (1) The Prophet (s) encouraged planting trees even if the Day of Judgment arrives, (2) Water waste is prohibited, (3) Pollution and destruction of nature is haram, (4) All creatures have rights, (5) Balance in consumption is emphasized. Environmental activism and conservation align perfectly with Islamic values.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['environment','stewardship','khalifah','conservation']},
{id:'mi065',cat:'misc',sub:'Environment',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the Islamic view on environmental protection?',a:'Islam\'s environmental ethic is comprehensive: humans are trustees of creation, not exploiters. Wasting resources (israf) is sinful. Contaminating water, air, and soil harms the community and is prohibited. Planting trees is rewarded as ongoing charity. Hunting endangered species, deforestation, and pollution violate the trust Allah placed in humanity. Muslims should be at the forefront of environmental protection.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['environment','trust','resources','pollution']},
{id:'mi066',cat:'misc',sub:'Pets',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules about keeping pets?',a:'Keeping animals as pets is permissible with conditions: (1) The animal must be properly fed and cared for, (2) Dogs are considered najis in Shia fiqh — keeping them inside the home requires extra attention to tahara rules, but keeping them for guarding, herding, or hunting is permissible, (3) Cats are clean and permissible to keep, (4) Fish, birds, and other animals are fine, (5) No animal should be confined in a way that causes suffering.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['pets','dogs','cats','permissible']},
{id:'mi067',cat:'misc',sub:'Photography',marja:'sistani',q:'Is photography permissible in Islam?',a:'Photography is permissible — it is not considered the same as the prohibited statues/idols referenced in hadith. Taking family photos, ID photos, documenting events, and professional photography are all fine. The concern arises with: immodest photography, photographing people without their consent, using photos to harm or humiliate others, and excessive vanity through selfies. The medium is permissible; the content and intent determine the ruling.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['photography','permissible','consent','modesty']},
{id:'mi068',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on tattoos?',a:'The ruling on tattoos is debated. They are not najis and do not prevent water from reaching the skin during ghusl (since the ink is under the skin). Some scholars consider them makruh (disliked) but not haram. Content matters: tattoos with Islamic content (names of Allah, verses) raise issues of respect, while tattoos with haram imagery are problematic regardless. The pain and health risks should also be considered.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['tattoos','debated','skin','makruh']},
{id:'mi069',cat:'misc',sub:'Tattoos',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on tattoos?',a:'Tattoos in themselves are not haram if the content is appropriate and the process does not cause significant harm. The ink being under the skin does not impede wudu or ghusl. However, tattoos with inappropriate content, those that cause self-harm, or those done in rebellion against social norms may be problematic. Permanent body modification should be approached thoughtfully.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['tattoos','permissible','wudu','content']},
{id:'mi070',cat:'misc',sub:'Sports',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the Islamic guidelines for sports and exercise?',a:'Physical fitness is encouraged in Islam — the Prophet (s) recommended swimming, archery, and horse riding. Guidelines: (1) Maintain proper covering, (2) Gender separation in activities where covering cannot be maintained, (3) No gambling on outcomes, (4) No activities with serious risk of injury or death, (5) Exercise should not override religious obligations (prayer times), (6) Competition should be friendly, not hostile. Being physically fit enables better worship and service.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['sports','exercise','fitness','covering']}
,
{id:'mi071',cat:'misc',sub:'Tawbah',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for sincere repentance (tawbah)?',a:'True repentance requires: (1) Genuine regret for the sin, (2) Immediate cessation of the sinful act, (3) Firm resolve not to repeat it, (4) If the sin involved others\' rights — restoring those rights (returning stolen property, seeking forgiveness), (5) If it involved missed religious duties — making them up (qada prayers, unpaid khums). Allah\'s mercy is infinite — the Quran says He forgives ALL sins for those who sincerely repent (39:53).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['tawbah','repentance','conditions','mercy']},
{id:'mi072',cat:'misc',sub:'Tawbah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the conditions for sincere repentance (tawbah)?',a:'Repentance (tawbah) has three pillars: regret, cessation, and resolve. For sins against others, a fourth is added: rectification of the wrong. Repentance should be immediate — delaying it is itself problematic. Allah loves those who repent and invites them back. Even major sins are forgivable through sincere tawbah. The door of repentance remains open until the moment of death or until the signs of the Day of Judgment appear.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['tawbah','pillars','immediate','forgiveness']},
{id:'mi073',cat:'misc',sub:'Istikhara',marja:'sistani',q:'What is istikhara and when should it be performed?',a:'Istikhara is seeking divine guidance when faced with a decision after rational analysis is inconclusive. Methods: (1) Prayer istikhara — pray two rakaat and ask Allah for guidance, (2) Quran istikhara — open the Quran after dua and interpret the verse, (3) Tasbih istikhara — using prayer beads. Important: istikhara supplements, not replaces, rational thinking. It should not be used for clearly right or wrong decisions or for matters with clear Islamic rulings.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['istikhara','guidance','methods','rational']},
{id:'mi074',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on smoking?',a:'Smoking that causes significant harm to health is haram based on the principle of self-harm prohibition. Given overwhelming medical evidence linking smoking to cancer, heart disease, and other serious conditions, most contemporary scholars consider it haram or strongly makruh. Starting to smoke is particularly condemned. Secondhand smoke that harms others adds another dimension of prohibition. Vaping and e-cigarettes follow the same health-based analysis.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['smoking','haram','health','self-harm']},
{id:'mi075',cat:'misc',sub:'Smoking',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on smoking?',a:'Given the established medical evidence of severe health risks, smoking is prohibited. The Islamic principle of preserving health and not causing self-harm clearly applies. Those addicted should make sincere effort to quit — addiction treatment is encouraged. Hookah and shisha carry the same ruling. The social normalization of smoking in some Muslim cultures does not change the religious ruling based on clear evidence of harm.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['smoking','prohibited','addiction','quit']},
{id:'mi076',cat:'misc',sub:'Education',marja:'sistani',q:'Is seeking secular education important in Islam?',a:'Seeking knowledge — both religious and worldly — is obligatory. The Prophet (s) said seeking knowledge is obligatory for every Muslim. Imam Ali (a) said knowledge is better than wealth. Science, medicine, engineering, technology, and other secular disciplines are not opposed to Islam — they are encouraged. Muslim civilization\'s golden age was built on combining religious knowledge with scientific excellence. Neglecting education contradicts Islamic values.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['education','secular','knowledge','obligatory']},
{id:'mi077',cat:'misc',sub:'Lying',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on lying?',a:'Lying is one of the most condemned sins in Islam. The Prophet (s) said: the signs of a hypocrite are three, and one is that they lie. However, limited exceptions exist: (1) To save a life, (2) To reconcile between quarreling parties, (3) In warfare deception, (4) Between spouses to maintain harmony (kind words, not deception in serious matters). Even in these exceptions, the minimum necessary should be said. Taqiyyah (dissimulation to protect life) has its own detailed rulings.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['lying','haram','exceptions','taqiyyah']},
{id:'mi078',cat:'misc',sub:'Racism',marja:'makarem',q:'What is Islam\'s position on racism?',a:'Islam categorically rejects racism. The Quran states that human diversity in colors and languages is a sign of Allah (30:22), and that the most noble before Allah is the most God-conscious, not the most racially pure (49:13). The Prophet\'s farewell sermon explicitly declared no Arab has superiority over a non-Arab. Racism contradicts the fundamental Islamic principle of human equality before God.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['racism','equality','quran','diversity']},
{id:'mi079',cat:'misc',sub:'Gratitude',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the importance of gratitude (shukr) in Islam?',a:'Gratitude is a fundamental Islamic value. The Quran promises: if you are grateful, I will increase you (14:7). Gratitude has three levels: (1) Of the heart — recognizing blessings come from Allah, (2) Of the tongue — praising and thanking Allah verbally, (3) Of the limbs — using blessings in obedience to Allah. Ingratitude is associated with disbelief (kufr literally means to cover/hide blessings). Being grateful to people is also emphasized.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['gratitude','shukr','levels','blessings']},
{id:'mi080',cat:'misc',sub:'Patience',marja:'khamenei',q:'What types of patience (sabr) does Islam teach?',a:'Three types of sabr: (1) Patience in worship — persisting in religious duties when difficult, (2) Patience in avoiding sin — resisting temptation, (3) Patience during hardship — enduring trials with faith. The Quran mentions sabr over 90 times. Patient believers receive immeasurable reward (39:10). Patience does not mean passivity — it means steadfastness while actively working to improve one\'s situation within Islamic boundaries.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['patience','sabr','types','active']}
,
{id:'mi200',cat:'misc',sub:'Dua',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the etiquettes of making dua (supplication)?',a:'Dua etiquettes: (1) Begin with praise of Allah and salawat on Muhammad and his family, (2) Face qiblah, (3) Raise hands, (4) Have wudu, (5) Be in a state of sincerity and humility, (6) Cry or appear as if crying, (7) Be persistent — do not give up, (8) Choose blessed times (last third of night, after obligatory prayers, Friday afternoon, Month of Ramadan), (9) Include others in your dua, (10) End with salawat. Have complete certainty that Allah hears and responds.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dua','etiquette','supplication','times']},
{id:'mi201',cat:'misc',sub:'Dua',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the etiquettes of making dua (supplication)?',a:'Effective dua requires: sincerity, trust in Allah\'s response, beginning with His praise and salawat, humility, persistence, choosing blessed times, and not limiting expectations. The Prophet (s) said: call upon Allah with certainty of response. Allah responds in three ways: granting the request, averting harm equivalent to the request, or storing the reward for the afterlife. Every dua is answered — sometimes in unexpected ways.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['dua','sincerity','response','trust']},
{id:'mi202',cat:'misc',sub:'Tawassul',marja:'sistani',q:'What is tawassul and is it permissible?',a:'Tawassul is seeking intercession through the Prophet (s) and the Ahlul Bayt (a) in one\'s supplications to Allah. It is permissible and recommended. The Quran encourages seeking means of approach (wasila) to Allah (5:35). Tawassul is not worship of the intermediary — it is asking them to intercede with Allah, who alone grants or denies. The Prophet and Imams are alive in barzakh and can hear and intercede.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['tawassul','intercession','permissible','wasila']},
{id:'mi203',cat:'misc',sub:'Tawassul',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is tawassul and is it permissible?',a:'Tawassul through the Prophet (s) and Imams (a) is a well-established practice with Quranic and hadith support. The Quran instructs seeking wasila (means of approach) to Allah. The souls of the Prophet and Imams are alive and can intercede. This is not shirk — the supplicant asks Allah through the intermediary\'s elevated status. Rejecting tawassul contradicts Quranic instruction and centuries of Muslim scholarly consensus.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['tawassul','quran','intercession','shirk']},
{id:'mi204',cat:'misc',sub:'Death',marja:'sistani',q:'What should one do when they feel death is approaching?',a:'The dying person should: (1) Face qiblah, (2) Recite the shahadatayn, (3) Ask for forgiveness, (4) Recite Quran (Surah Yasin is recommended), (5) Express their will and debts to those present, (6) Have good opinion of Allah\'s mercy, (7) Be in a state of acceptance. Those present should: remind them of Allah\'s mercy, recite Quran near them, moisten their lips, ensure they face qiblah, and not cry loudly near them.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dying','preparation','shahadah','qiblah']},
{id:'mi205',cat:'misc',sub:'Death',marja:'khamenei',q:'What should one do when they feel death is approaching?',a:'The dying should be helped to face qiblah. Those present should recite the shahadatayn and names of the 12 Imams for the dying person to hear and repeat. Surah Yasin and other Quran should be recited. The atmosphere should be calm and spiritual. Having good thoughts about Allah at the moment of death is crucial. The Prophet (s) said: let none of you die except while having good opinion of Allah.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['dying','imams','quran','mercy']}
,
{id:'mi206',cat:'misc',sub:'Muharram',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the significance of Muharram mourning?',a:'Mourning Imam Husayn (a) during Muharram is one of the most important Shia practices. It preserves the message of Karbala — standing against tyranny, preferring death with dignity over life with humiliation. Recommended acts: attending majalis (gatherings), reciting elegies, crying for Husayn (a), serving food (nazri), and reflecting on the principles of justice. The Imams (a) strongly encouraged keeping the memory of Karbala alive.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['muharram','mourning','husayn','karbala']},
{id:'mi207',cat:'misc',sub:'Muharram',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the significance of Muharram mourning?',a:'Muharram commemorations serve vital functions: preserving the revolutionary message of Imam Husayn (a), building community solidarity, educating about Islamic values of justice, and providing a model for standing against oppression. Mourning should be dignified and educational — focusing on the lessons of Karbala rather than just emotional displays. The rituals should attract people to Islam, not repel them through practices that distort the message.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['muharram','revolution','education','dignity']},
{id:'mi208',cat:'misc',sub:'Ziyarah',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the significance of visiting the shrines of the Imams?',a:'Ziyarah (visitation) of the Imams\' shrines is highly recommended and carries immense reward. The Prophet (s) and Imams (a) specifically encouraged visiting Imam Husayn\'s shrine in Karbala — some narrations equate it to visiting Allah\'s throne. Ziyarah strengthens faith, connects one with the Ahlul Bayt (a), and provides spiritual renewal. Even ziyarah from a distance (reciting the ziyarah text from afar) carries great reward.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['ziyarah','shrines','imams','reward']},
{id:'mi209',cat:'misc',sub:'Ziyarah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the significance of visiting the shrines of the Imams?',a:'Ziyarah of the holy shrines is a pillar of Shia spiritual life. Visiting Imam Husayn (a) in Karbala, Imam Rida (a) in Mashhad, and other Imams\' shrines is a profoundly rewarding act. The visitor should: perform ghusl, wear clean clothes, recite the designated ziyarah text, pray two rakaat, make dua, and reflect on the Imam\'s life and sacrifice. The spiritual atmosphere of these blessed places elevates the soul.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ziyarah','karbala','mashhad','spiritual']},
{id:'mi210',cat:'misc',sub:'Bidah',marja:'sistani',q:'What constitutes bidah (innovation) in religion?',a:'Bidah is introducing into religion something that has no basis in Quran, Sunnah, or the teachings of the Ahlul Bayt (a) while claiming it is part of the religion. Not every new practice is bidah — using microphones for adhan, printing Quran, and online Islamic classes are new methods for existing obligations, not innovations in religion. True bidah is declaring something obligatory or prohibited without divine authority.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['bidah','innovation','religion','new practices']},
{id:'mi211',cat:'misc',sub:'Bidah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What constitutes bidah (innovation) in religion?',a:'Bidah is attributing to Islam what is not from Islam. It is not simply doing something new — it is specifically claiming religious authority for practices without divine basis. Technology, modern educational methods, and contemporary solutions to Islamic problems are not bidah if they serve established Islamic objectives. Bidah occurs when someone changes the content of religion — adding obligations or removing established ones without authority.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['bidah','innovation','technology','authority']}
,
{id:'mi300',cat:'misc',sub:'Magic',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on magic and sorcery?',a:'Learning, practicing, and seeking magic (sihr) is haram. This includes: black magic, spells, talismans for evil purposes, and seeking help from jinn. The Quran condemns those who practice magic (2:102). Seeking out magicians or fortune-tellers is also prohibited. However, ruqya (Quranic recitation for healing and protection) is encouraged and different from magic. If someone is affected by magic, the remedy is through Quran, dua, and legitimate Islamic practices.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['magic','sorcery','haram','ruqya']},
{id:'mi301',cat:'misc',sub:'Magic',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on magic and sorcery?',a:'Magic and sorcery are categorically haram — the Quran explicitly condemns them. Seeking magical services, visiting fortune-tellers, using ouija boards, and attempting to communicate with jinn are all prohibited. Amulets with Quranic verses for protection are permissible, but those containing unknown symbols or non-Islamic content should be avoided. The best protection is through Quran recitation, dua, and reliance on Allah (tawakkul).',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['magic','fortune-telling','amulets','protection']},
{id:'mi302',cat:'misc',sub:'Interest',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic view on taking interest-based loans?',a:'Taking a loan on interest is haram from both sides — the lender and borrower. However, in cases of genuine necessity (basic housing, essential medical treatment) where no Islamic alternative exists, some scholars permit it under the principle of necessity (darurah). The necessity must be real, not merely for convenience or luxury. Muslims should exhaust Islamic financing options first and minimize riba-based transactions as much as possible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['interest','loans','necessity','minimize']},
{id:'mi303',cat:'misc',sub:'Interest',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the Islamic view on taking interest-based loans?',a:'Riba (interest) is one of the most severely condemned sins in the Quran — Allah declares war on those who practice it (2:279). However, necessity can permit what is otherwise forbidden. If someone genuinely needs a loan for essential needs and only interest-bearing options exist, it may be taken with the intention to seek forgiveness and minimize the involvement. Building Islamic financial alternatives is a community responsibility.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['riba','condemned','necessity','alternatives']},
{id:'mi304',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'sistani',q:'What forms of gambling are prohibited?',a:'All gambling (qimar) is haram — this includes: casino games, lotteries, sports betting, card games for money, dice games for money, raffle tickets where money is wagered, online gambling, and any game where participants risk money on uncertain outcomes. Even organizing or facilitating gambling (running a casino, selling lottery tickets) is prohibited. Prize-based competitions based on skill (not chance) are different and generally permissible.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['gambling','lottery','betting','prohibited']},
{id:'mi305',cat:'misc',sub:'Gambling',marja:'khamenei',q:'What forms of gambling are prohibited?',a:'The Quran places gambling alongside alcohol as a major sin (5:90). This encompasses: all games of chance involving money, lotteries (government or private), poker and casino games, sports betting, online gambling platforms, and even friendly card games for money. The prohibition extends to: operating gambling establishments, developing gambling software, and promoting gambling. Skill-based competitions with prizes from a third party (not the participants) are different.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['gambling','quran','all forms','software']},
{id:'mi306',cat:'misc',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the difference between backbiting (ghibah) and slander (buhtan)?',a:'Ghibah (backbiting) is saying something true about someone behind their back that they would dislike. Buhtan (slander) is saying something false about someone. Both are major sins, but buhtan is worse as it combines backbiting with lying. The Quran compares backbiting to eating the flesh of your dead brother (49:12). Repentance for ghibah ideally includes seeking forgiveness from the person. For buhtan, one must also correct the false information.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['ghibah','buhtan','slander','repentance']},
{id:'mi307',cat:'misc',sub:'Backbiting',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the difference between backbiting (ghibah) and slander (buhtan)?',a:'Ghibah: mentioning a true negative quality of someone in their absence. Buhtan: attributing a false negative quality to someone. Both are severe sins. Ghibah is compared to eating the dead in the Quran. Buhtan is even worse — combining the sin of backbiting with lying. In the digital age, this includes: gossiping online, sharing embarrassing true stories, writing false negative reviews, and spreading unverified rumors on social media.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ghibah','buhtan','digital','rumors']},
{id:'mi308',cat:'misc',sub:'Children\'s Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What does Islam say about child labor and exploitation?',a:'Islam prohibits exploitation of children. Children have the right to education, play, and a safe upbringing. Child labor that deprives children of education, harms their health, or interferes with their physical and mental development is forbidden. Light, age-appropriate tasks that teach responsibility are fine. The Prophet (s) was deeply compassionate toward children and condemned those who burden them beyond their capacity.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['children','labor','exploitation','education']},
{id:'mi309',cat:'misc',sub:'Children\'s Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What does Islam say about child labor and exploitation?',a:'Children are a trust from Allah. Exploiting their labor, depriving them of education, or subjecting them to physical or emotional harm are grave sins. Islam mandates providing children with: a nurturing environment, quality education, religious training, physical safety, and emotional support. Communities must develop systems to protect vulnerable children. Any form of child trafficking, abuse, or exploitation is among the most heinous crimes.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['children','protection','trust','trafficking']},
{id:'mi310',cat:'misc',sub:'Almsgiving',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the etiquette of giving and receiving charity?',a:'For the giver: give secretly when possible (right hand shouldn\'t know what left gives), don\'t follow charity with reminders or hurt (Quran 2:264), give from good quality possessions, give with humility (the receiver is doing you a favor by accepting), and don\'t look down on small amounts. For the receiver: accept with gratitude, use it for legitimate needs, and make dua for the giver. The reward of charity is in the intention, not the amount.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['charity','etiquette','secret','humility']},
{id:'mi311',cat:'misc',sub:'Almsgiving',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the etiquette of giving and receiving charity?',a:'Charity etiquette: give sincerely for Allah (not recognition), prefer secret giving, choose the most deserving recipients, give from what you love (Quran 3:92), maintain the recipient\'s dignity, never publicize their need, and be consistent. The Prophet (s) said: the upper hand (giving) is better than the lower hand (receiving). Regular small charity is better than occasional large amounts. Digital charitable giving through trusted platforms is a valid modern method.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['charity','dignity','consistent','digital']},
{id:'mi312',cat:'misc',sub:'Neighbors',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on causing noise disturbance to neighbors?',a:'Causing unnecessary noise disturbance to neighbors is prohibited as it violates their right to peaceful living. This includes: loud music or gatherings at night, construction noise during rest hours, loud arguments, and persistent noise from equipment. The Prophet (s) emphasized neighbors\' rights so heavily that he said: whoever\'s neighbor is not safe from their harm has not truly believed. Consideration for neighbors is a fundamental Islamic value.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['noise','neighbors','disturbance','prohibited']},
{id:'mi313',cat:'misc',sub:'Humor',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the Islamic view on humor and joking?',a:'Appropriate humor is part of a balanced personality — the Prophet (s) himself joked but never said anything untrue. Islamic humor guidelines: no lying (even as a joke), no mocking others, no vulgar or obscene content, no jokes about religion or sacred matters, no humor that hurts people\'s feelings, and moderation (too much joking reduces dignity). Clean, kind humor that brings joy without harming anyone is a positive social trait.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['humor','joking','prophet','appropriate']}
];